⏺ Yes, M4 is solved first. Let me trace exactly what happens and where the M5 overlaps come from. 
 
  Layer order — by_layer is a std::map<int, ...>, so layers iterate in ascending key order: M4 (4) → M5 (5) → M6 (6). (nuts.cpp:438) 
 
  The causal chain: 
 
  1. M4 solved first. The H trunks of all Z_VHV bundles share the [80, 420] Hanan interval and are packed together. With 55 bundles competing, many H trunks end up displaced significantly from their 
  nominal y=250. 
  2. Post-M4 span adjustment fires. For each placed H trunk T, rev_conn_map says "adjust connected V stubs". The V stubs (M5, not yet placed) have their span_lo or span_hi SET to T's stripe edge — so a 
  V1 stub whose H trunk ended up at y=205 gets its span_lo pulled down from 250 to ~203.5. 
  3. M5 solve runs with extended spans. The V stubs now have longer spans than the topology originally specified. More V segments are active simultaneously across overlapping routing-direction ranges, 
  saturating the Hanan x-cells. The solver can't fit them all without collision. 
 
  What post-M5 adjustment does NOT do — this is the important part of your hypothesis. After M5 segments are placed, the span adjustment loop fires again, but it only ever touches M4 segments (visually 
  trimming H trunk spans to meet where V stubs landed). It never modifies another M5 segment's position or span, because in rev_conn_map, V stubs map to their H trunk, never to a peer V stub. So post-M5 
   span adjustment does not create new M5 overlaps — those overlaps already exist at the moment solve_layer(M5_segs) returns. 
 
  Root cause: M4 packing displaces H trunks → V spans grow → M5 channel saturates. The 60 M5 overlaps are a capacity problem, not a span-adjustment artifact. The channel needs to be wide enough that M4 
  packing keeps H trunks close to nominal, keeping V spans short. The 340-unit channel is just barely under-provisioned for this seed. 
 
